The construction $G=(k-2)K_{k-1}$ (disjoint union of $k-2$ cliques, each of size $k-1$) has $(k-1)(k-2)$ vertices. Any $k$-vertex set must meet some clique in at least 2 vertices, so the induced subgraph is a disjoint union of cliques containing either a triangle or at least two disjoint edges. Such a graph has maximum degree $\le k-2$ (hence does not contain $K_{1,k-1}$) and is not a subgraph of $K_{1,k-1}$ (since all edges of a star share a vertex). Thus every induced $k$-subgraph is incomparable with $K_{1,k-1}$, so $G$ is exact and $f(K_{1,k-1})\ge (k-1)(k-2)$.

For the upper bound, let $G$ be $K_{1,k-1}$-exact. If some induced $k$-subgraph equals $K_{1,k-1}$, its center has $k-1$ independent neighbors; any additional vertex forces a $k$-set inducing either a proper subgraph of the star (comparable, forbidden) or a non-star supergraph. If no induced $k$-subgraph equals $K_{1,k-1}$, then every $k$-set induces an incomparable graph, so $G$ contains no induced $kK_1$ (independent set of size $k$) and no induced $K_2\cup(k-2)K_1$. These constraints force $G$ to be a disjoint union of at most $k-2$ cliques, each of size at most $k-1$ (a $k$-clique would properly contain the star). Hence $|V(G)|\le (k-2)(k-1)$.

\boxed{(k-1)(k-2)}

